<clickhouse>
    <!--
      System logs, trimmed to the ones anything actually reads.

      WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. After an unclean shutdown the server came back pinned at its memory
      ceiling and stayed there with no queries, no merges and no mutations running - 5.40 GiB
      allocated against 68.79 MiB that ClickHouse's own tracker admitted to. The log said what was
      holding it:

        Failed to flush system log system.asynchronous_metric_log with 4200 entries up to
        offset 106327: (total) memory limit exceeded

      That is a feedback loop, and it cannot break itself. Memory is full, so the flush fails;
      the flush failed, so the buffer keeps the rows; the buffer grows, so memory is fuller. The
      server was not busy - it was holding a log of how busy it had been.

      These five had also written about 7,000 parts between them during the restart loop, which is
      most of the 9,007 parts in the store and none of the data anybody asked for.

      query_metric_log is here for the same reason metric_log is: its DDL alone is 143 KB of
      column definitions, and a table that wide costs more per part than the rows in it.

      WHAT IS KEPT. query_log and part_log stay: query_log is what benchmarks read for rows_read
      and timings when a measurement needs the server's own accounting rather than the client's.
      The ones removed here are diagnostics for a busy production cluster, and on a 6 GiB
      development container they cost more than they tell.

      IN PRODUCTION, turn metric_log and asynchronous_metric_log back on and give them a TTL.
      They are genuinely useful when there is memory to hold them and somebody reading them.
    -->
    <asynchronous_metric_log remove="1"/>
    <metric_log remove="1"/>
    <text_log remove="1"/>
    <error_log remove="1"/>
    <query_metric_log remove="1"/>
</clickhouse>
